<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for a hardened-runtime build.

  Present and complete now, though nothing signs today: the hardened runtime is only applied when
  there is a signing identity, and an entitlements file written on the day the certificate arrives
  is an entitlements file written in a hurry. Every entry below is here because Electron or this
  app actually needs it, and the two that are *absent* are the interesting part.

  What is here:
    - allow-jit / allow-unsigned-executable-memory / allow-dyld-environment-variables
      V8 needs all three. Without them a hardened Electron app crashes on launch.
    - network.client — the Mac dials out to the phone (locked decision #2).
    - files.user-selected.read-write — the file picker for "send to phone" and the CSV export.
    - files.downloads.read-write — pulls land in ~/Downloads/ConnectSuite.

  What is deliberately NOT here:
    - com.apple.security.app-sandbox. This is a Developer ID build for direct distribution, not a
      Mac App Store one. Sandboxing it would require entitlements for the local network listener
      behaviour and a container-relative Downloads path, and neither has been designed. If this
      ever goes to the App Store that is a separate piece of work, not a line to add here.
    - network.server. The Mac never listens; the phone binds every socket. Asking for an
      entitlement this app does not use is the permission equivalent of a greyed control that will
      never light up.

  ## cs.disable-library-validation — added 2026-09-03, and it is load-bearing TODAY

  Without it **the packaged app does not launch at all**, measured:

      dyld: Library not loaded: @rpath/Electron Framework.framework/Electron Framework
      Reason: ... mapping process and mapped file (non-platform) have different Team IDs

  `hardenedRuntime: true` turns on library validation, which requires every loaded library to carry
  the *same* Team ID as the process. An ad-hoc signature has **no** Team ID — `TeamIdentifier=not
  set` on both the app and `Electron Framework.framework` — and macOS does not treat two absent team
  IDs as a match. So the inside-out ad-hoc signing that fixed D12's "unassessable" verdict produced
  a bundle that verifies deep, assesses as `rejected` rather than erroring, and cannot be run.

  **The day there is a Developer ID this entitlement should be deleted**, not kept: with a real
  identity every nested component shares one Team ID, library validation passes on its own, and this
  line then only weakens a protection that would otherwise be working. It is here for the ad-hoc
  case and its removal belongs in the same change that fills in `identity:`.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
  <true/>
  <!-- Required while the build is ad-hoc signed. See the header; delete it when signing for real. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.files.downloads.read-write</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
